   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Perceived Crisis-Management Training Adequacy and Wellbeing Indicators Among Seafarers During the Red Sea Crisis: An Exploratory Survey</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Perceived Crisis-Management Training Adequacy and Wellbeing Indicators Among Seafarers During the Red Sea Crisis: An Exploratory Survey</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>geopolitical risk; seafarer wellbeing; supply chain resilience; maritime training; security incident exposure; Red Sea crisis; STCW; human factor</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <dc:type>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Text</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:type>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Global shipping carries over 80% of world trade</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Global shipping carries over 80% of world trade</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">yet existing supply-chain-resilience theory models resilience at the level of the firm</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">yet existing supply-chain-resilience theory models resilience at the level of the firm</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">node and network</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">node and network</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">without treating the workforce operating those nodes as a variable. This exploratory study addresses that gap using the 2023--2026 Red Sea/Houthi crisis as its empirical setting. A pilot-scale</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">without treating the workforce operating those nodes as a variable. This exploratory study addresses that gap using the 2023--2026 Red Sea/Houthi crisis as its empirical setting. A pilot-scale</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">cross-sectional online survey of 64 serving seafarers combined closed-ended items with qualitative content analysis of two open-text questions. Seafarers who had witnessed or been directly involved in a security incident rated STCW crisis-management training as less adequate for conflict-area risk than those without such exposure (Mann--Whitney U = 227.0</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">cross-sectional online survey of 64 serving seafarers combined closed-ended items with qualitative content analysis of two open-text questions. Seafarers who had witnessed or been directly involved in a security incident rated STCW crisis-management training as less adequate for conflict-area risk than those without such exposure (Mann--Whitney U = 227.0</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">p = 0.007</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">p = 0.007</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">r = 0.34)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">r = 0.34)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">the only comparison that remained significant after Holm adjustment within its family of tests; Red Sea transit or Cape of Good Hope rerouting without an incident was not associated with lower ratings. Open-text responses identified situational information and realistic scenario-based drills</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">the only comparison that remained significant after Holm adjustment within its family of tests; Red Sea transit or Cape of Good Hope rerouting without an incident was not associated with lower ratings. Open-text responses identified situational information and realistic scenario-based drills</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">rather than generic compliance training</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">rather than generic compliance training</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">as the specific gap</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">as the specific gap</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and a minority questioned seafarersâ€™ right to decline a high-risk voyage. Because fatigue and psychological distress could be captured only through single</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and a minority questioned seafarersâ€™ right to decline a high-risk voyage. Because fatigue and psychological distress could be captured only through single</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">non-validated items</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">non-validated items</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">findings on these outcomes are indicative only. Building on these results</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">findings on these outcomes are indicative only. Building on these results</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">the study proposes</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">the study proposes</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">as exploratory propositions for future testing rather than established relationships</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">as exploratory propositions for future testing rather than established relationships</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">two human-factor pathways through which geopolitical risk may affect operational resilience: a training-competence pathway and a retention/psychological-contract pathway. The study contributes hypothesis-generating evidence</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">two human-factor pathways through which geopolitical risk may affect operational resilience: a training-competence pathway and a retention/psychological-contract pathway. The study contributes hypothesis-generating evidence</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">gathered directly from an affected workforce</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">gathered directly from an affected workforce</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and derives practical and policy recommendations for STCW training content and seafarer consent protections.</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and derives practical and policy recommendations for STCW training content and seafarer consent protections.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
